capability random/generator  a seeded generator whose state the caller holds, the same seed answering the same numbers
  heroes  random.Generator
  go      rand.PCG, rand.NewPCG, rand.PCG.Seed, rand.Rand, rand.New, rand.Source
  rust    random::Rng
  python  random.Random, random.seed, random.getstate, random.setstate
  note    PCG with 128 bits of state and the DXSM output, Go's `PCG`; its multiplier and increment were read back from three states Go's `MarshalBinary` showed, and its first numbers from four seeds answered as Go's do. The state is a record built directly, so seeding, getting and setting the state are building and reading it; Python's Mersenne Twister is not reproduced

capability random/system-seed  a generator seeded from the system's generator for keys
  heroes  random.from_system
  go      -
  rust    random::random
  python  random.Random, random.seed
  note    Python seeds from `os.urandom` when given no seed; Go's top-level functions do the same with a generator of their own, which a program cannot hold

capability random/u64  the next number, any of its type as likely
  heroes  random.next_u64
  go      rand.PCG.Uint64, rand.Uint64, rand.Rand.Uint64, rand.Int64, rand.Rand.Int64, rand.Uint32, rand.Rand.Uint32, rand.Int32, rand.Rand.Int32, rand.Int, rand.Rand.Int, rand.Uint, rand.Rand.Uint
  rust    random::Distribution
  python  random.getrandbits
  note    a narrower or signed number is `below` or `int_in`; Python's `getrandbits(k)` is `below` of 2^k

capability random/below  a number below a bound, without bias
  heroes  random.below
  go      rand.Uint64N, rand.Rand.Uint64N, rand.Uint32N, rand.Rand.Uint32N, rand.UintN, rand.Rand.UintN, rand.N, rand.Rand.N
  rust    -
  python  random.randrange
  note    Lemire's method, and the low bits for a power of two, as Go's `Uint64N` draws: its numbers answered from the same state; a bound of 0 aborts, as Go panics

capability random/range  a whole number in a range, without bias
  heroes  random.int_in
  go      rand.Int64N, rand.Rand.Int64N, rand.Int32N, rand.Rand.Int32N, rand.IntN, rand.Rand.IntN
  rust    -
  python  random.randint, random.randrange
  note    `to` is left out as the built-in `range` leaves it, where Python's `randint` includes it; Go's `Int64N(n)` is `int_in(from: 0, to: n)` and answered alike

capability random/float  a float in [0, 1)
  heroes  random.next_f64
  go      rand.Float64, rand.Rand.Float64, rand.Float32, rand.Rand.Float32
  rust    -
  python  random.random
  note    the low 53 bits of a number over 2^53, as Go's `Float64` takes them, and its numbers answered; a float in another range is `a + (b - a) * next_f64`, which is Python's `uniform`

capability random/bytes  bytes from a seeded generator
  heroes  random.next_bytes, random.next_bytes_chacha8
  go      rand.ChaCha8.Read
  rust    -
  python  random.randbytes
  note    `next_bytes_chacha8` gives Go's `ChaCha8.Read`'s bytes, a number's bytes a read did not use kept for the next read and kept across a `Uint64` between, as Go answered on 2026-11-17; PCG's `next_bytes` keeps none, Go's `PCG` having no `Read`

capability random/shuffle  a list put in an order drawn at random
  heroes  random.shuffle
  go      rand.Shuffle, rand.Rand.Shuffle, rand.Perm, rand.Rand.Perm
  rust    -
  python  random.shuffle
  note    Fisher and Yates's method from the last element down; Go's own puts the elements in another order from the same state, so the tests hold the order's spread and not Go's order. Go's `Perm(n)` is `shuffle` of `range(from: 0, to: n)`

capability random/choose  an element drawn from a list
  heroes  random.choose, random.choose_chacha8
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  random.choice
  note    an empty list fails `invalid`, where Python raises

capability random/secure-choose  an element drawn from a list by the system's generator for keys
  heroes  random.secure_choose
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  secrets.choice
  note    Lemire's method over eight bytes at a time of `os.random_bytes`, as `secure_below` draws, so no element is favoured; an empty list fails `invalid`, where Python raises

capability random/sample  elements drawn without replacement
  heroes  random.sample
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  random.sample
  note    Python's `counts` argument is a list holding each element as many times

capability random/weighted  an element drawn by weights
  heroes  random.choose_weighted
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  random.choices
  note    Python's `choices` draws `k` with replacement, which is `choose_weighted` called `k` times, and takes cumulative weights too, which are not offered here

capability random/secure  a number below a bound from the system's generator for keys
  heroes  random.secure_below
  go      -
  rust    random::SystemRng
  python  secrets.randbelow, secrets.randbits, random.SystemRandom
  note    random bytes for a key are `os.random_bytes`, Python's `token_bytes`; a token written as text is `secure_token_hex` or `secure_token_base64_url`

capability random/distributions  numbers drawn by a distribution other than the uniform one
  heroes  pending
  go      rand.NormFloat64, rand.Rand.NormFloat64, rand.ExpFloat64, rand.Rand.ExpFloat64, rand.Zipf, rand.NewZipf, rand.Zipf.Uint64
  rust    -
  python  random.gauss, random.normalvariate, random.lognormvariate, random.expovariate, random.vonmisesvariate, random.gammavariate, random.betavariate, random.paretovariate, random.weibullvariate, random.triangular, random.binomialvariate, random.uniform
  note    each needs its own method and a test by its own moments; `uniform` is a line of arithmetic over `next_f64`

capability random/chacha8  a generator whose numbers cannot be told from the ones before
  heroes  random.ChaCha8, random.from_seed_chacha8, random.from_system_chacha8, random.next_u64_chacha8, random.next_f64_chacha8, random.below_chacha8, random.int_in_chacha8, random.shuffle_chacha8, random.choose_chacha8, random.sample_chacha8, random.choose_weighted_chacha8
  go      rand.ChaCha8, rand.NewChaCha8, rand.ChaCha8.Seed, rand.ChaCha8.Uint64
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    Go's ChaCha8rand: ChaCha with eight rounds, four blocks a group interleaved word by word, the key added back to its own words alone, and the last 32 bytes of every fourth group made the next key, 124 numbers a key; the first thousand numbers of four seeds answered as Go's did on 2026-11-17, seven changes of key crossed, and Go's `Uint64N`, `Float64` and `Int64N` through `rand.New` drew the same. Go's `Seed` is a new `from_seed_chacha8`. Without interfaces a draw cannot take either generator, so each draw of `Generator` has its `_chacha8` twin by the same method, as `hash` names `start32` and `start64`. Two million numbers took 0.9 s against PCG's 1.5 s, plain build, measured that day

capability random/token  a token of random bytes written as text, for a link, a key or a session
  heroes  random.secure_token_hex, random.secure_token_base64_url
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  secrets.token_hex, secrets.token_urlsafe, secrets.token_bytes, secrets.DEFAULT_ENTROPY
  note    `token_bytes` is `os.random_bytes`; hexadecimal in lower case and base 64's URL-safe alphabet with no padding, as Python wrote them on 2026-11-17; the count of bytes is always given, where Python's default is `DEFAULT_ENTROPY`, 32 bytes, which the documentation names; `random` may not use `bytes`, of its own layer, so the two encodings are written again privately for this use

dropped state-bytes  Go's saving of a generator to bytes and back; a `Generator` is two numbers a program keeps as it keeps any record
  go      rand.PCG.AppendBinary, rand.PCG.MarshalBinary, rand.PCG.UnmarshalBinary, rand.ChaCha8.AppendBinary, rand.ChaCha8.MarshalBinary, rand.ChaCha8.UnmarshalBinary
  rust    -
  python  -

dropped constants  numbers Python's implementation keeps for its own distributions
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  random.BPF, random.LOG4, random.NV_MAGICCONST, random.RECIP_BPF, random.SG_MAGICCONST, random.TWOPI, random.main
